  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to break this down. The user wants a probability forecast for a solution to at least one of the Millennium Prize Problems by certain deadlines, with AI involvement. The forecast date is 2025-04-29, so I need to consider possibilities based on knowledge up to that point.\n\nFirst, the problems open then: Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, Yang-Mills. Poincar\u00e9 is already solved, so excluded. The event is a correct solution published by a deadline, with AI contributing a new argument. Even partial progress from AI doesn't count unless it's part of a full solution.\n\nI need to assign probabilities by 2026, 2030, etc. They must be non-decreasing and between 0-1. Also, account for the event never happening.\n\nStarting with 2026: That's less than two years from the forecast date. Historically, these problems have been unsolved for decades. Even with AI, solving one in two years seems extremely unlikely. If there were major breakthroughs, maybe in AI-assisted theorem proving, but as of 2025, AI's role in rigorous proofs is still emerging. Most of these problems are purely mathematical and require deep insight, not amenable to brute-force computation. Probability here might be 0.01 or something very small.\n\nFor 2030: Science.gov mentions AI advancements up to 2025. Maybe in five years (2030), with more years of AI development, there's a slight chance. But again, these are hard. Suppose AI can generate plausible conjectures or check complex arguments, but the full proof for these might still be out of reach. Maybe 0.1? Still low but higher than 2026.\n\n2035 is ten years out. AI might better assist mathematicians through 2035. For example, if AI helps discover a key theorem or counterexample. However, the problems are interconnected with deeper mathematical structures that aren't easily computable. Maybe 0.2.\n\n2040 (15 years): With continued AI development, like self-taught mathematical AI or