it doesn't count. The question is the chance that AI contributes a new step to a solution.\n\nSo the probability should be lower than a human-only solution. However, if AI becomes an essential tool for mathematicians in the coming decades, it might contribute.\n\nFor example, P vs NP is considered one of the hardest. If AI helps in exploring new complexity classes or finding a clever polynomial-time algorithm for an NP-complete problem (unlikely). Similarly, a direct AI breakthrough would be possible in the long term but seems very hard even in 2050.\n\nNavier-Stokes equation existence might be more numerical or categorical, but smoothness is challenging. Hodge conjecture is algebraic geometry, which is traditionally pure math and not algorithmic (AI unlikely to help much directly). Yang-Mills is physics-oriented but also tricky.\n\nThus, even with AI, changing various fields, the probability remains low but increases with time. So the probabilities might be:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.05\n\n2035: 0.10\n\n2040: 0.20\n\n2050: 0.30\n\nThat seems plausible, non-decreasing and increasing with time. But given the difficulty, maybe even 2050 is still low. Suppose if AI gets much better, maybe 0.5 by 2050? But user says \"account for the possibility the event never occurs\". So I can't be too high. Let's see. Making the 2050 probability 0.35.\n\nBut maybe the numbers should reflect a low overall. For example:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.01 (maybe too little change? But the 2030 deadline is 5 years away, so maybe 0.02?)\n\nBut the user mentioned Science.gov information up to 2025. If there's no mention of AI solving these problems yet, the increments should be cautious.\n\nHence, perhaps:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.02\n\n2035: 0.03\n\n2040: 0.05\n\n2050: 0.08\n\nWait, but this is very low. Maybe the user expects a higher chance. Wait, the original direction: the probabilities are for the occurrence by that date. For example, by 2050, over 25 years, perhaps there's a 10% chance. But if the user is building in the possibility that none ever come, the numbers can't be too high.\n\nAnother approach: consider others have predicted such things. But I don't have current data. So based on just my knowledge up to 2025, I'd say the chance is small, but increases with time.\n\nSo:\n\n2026: 0.001\n\n2030: 0.003\n\n2035: 0.007\n\n2040: 0.01\n\n2050: 0.02\n\nBut the user might think these numbers are too low, but the instructions are to give my best estimates despite uncertainty.\n\nAlternatively, maybe for 2050, 0.1 (10%).\n\nBut considering all factors, I think 2026 is 0.01, 2030 0.02, 2035 0.03, 2040 0.05, 2050 0.10. But since these numbers must be non-decreasing and as such, the JSON will look like that.\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.02,\n  \"2035\": 0.03,\n  \"2040\": 0.05,\n  \"2050\": 0.10\n}",
